          %p
            All this may be summed up by saying that a natural numbers object is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+algebra"} initial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+for+an+endofunctor"} algebra for the endofunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ↦
                  %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \mapsto 1 + X
            (the functor underlying the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/maybe+monad"}> maybe monad
            ”). Equivalently, it is an initial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+for+an+endo-profunctor"} algebra for the endo-profunctor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Hom
                      %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %msub
                      %mi Hom
                      %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom_E(1,=) \times Hom_E(-,=)
          %p
            By the universal property, the natural numbers object is unique up to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C, D
              be cartesian closed categories, and suppose
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              has a natural numbers object
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi z
                      %mo :
                      %mn 1
                      %mo →
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi s
                      %mo :
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo →
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbb{N}, z: 1 \to \mathbb{N}, s: \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N})
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo :
                    %mi D
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L: D \to C
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
              that preserves the terminal object, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi L
                    %mi z
                    %mo ,
                    %mi L
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (L \mathbb{N}, L z, L s)
              is a natural numbers object in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
            The proof is straightforward. It follows for example that the left adjoint part
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^\ast
            of a geometric morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo :
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^\ast \dashv f_\ast: E \to F
            between toposes with natural numbers objects preserves the natural numbers object, and also that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+quasitopos"} Grothendieck quasitopos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            presented by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi J
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C, J)
            has a natural numbers object, since the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+subcategory"} reflection functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L: Set^{C^{op}} \to Q
            preserves finite products and the terminal object in particular.
          %h3#in_a_closed_symmetric_monoidal_category In a closed symmetric monoidal category
          %p
            One could generalize the above definition of a natural numbers object to any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+objects+in+a+monoidal+category"} pointed objects in a symmetric monoidal category
            are represented by morphisms out of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+unit"} tensor unit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+unit"} tensor unit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            is
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
                in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %li
              %p equipped with
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    a
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi z
                          %mo :
                          %mn 1
                          %mo →
                          %mi ℕ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z :1 \to \mathbb{N}
                    from the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+unit"} tensor unit
                    = succeed ";" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mn 1
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
                %li
                  %p
                    a
